{"id":"paid-after-the-end","title":"Still paid after the term ran out","question":"Which contracts were still being paid a year or more after the end date on record, the nearest thing in the data to a holdover?","finding":"119 contracts show payments in a fiscal year that began at least twelve months after their recorded end date, $20.7 million in all. 29 are construction contracts ($4.9 million), where late final payments are routine. The other 90 hold $15.9 million; 31 are grants. They sit with City Administrator (15); Public Health (15); Mayor (10); Human Rights Commission (7). Set against $71.77 billion in contracts, this is small: the table does not show widespread payment on expired contracts.","how":"Each payment line carries a contract number, a voucher number and a fiscal-year label, but no date of payment. The label is not always the year the payment was made, so the year is read from the voucher number, which is one running sequence. A contract is listed when lines under its number were paid in a fiscal year that began twelve months or more after the end date in the Supplier Contracts table, and those payments total $10,000 or more. Read from the label instead, the same test would list 177 contracts and $98.5 million; the difference is payments filed under a later year than the one they were made in. Construction contracts are counted in the finding but not listed. Largest amount first.","limits":" A final invoice, a retained sum released after close-out, a settlement of a disputed bill and a payment keyed to an old contract number by mistake all look like this. The end date is today’s: where a department extended a contract and updated the date, nothing shows, and where it extended without updating, an extension shows as a late payment. A lease that runs on month to month after its term does so under the lease’s own holdover clause. What would settle a row: the contract’s amendment record and the invoice dates. The payment file labels each line with a fiscal year that is not always the year the payment was issued: some earlier payments, mostly on purchase orders in multi-year capital funds, are filed under a later year, and the Controller's own published figures follow that label. Payments are dated here by voucher number, a single running sequence, which moves $5.52 billion of $119.3 billion (4.6%) to an earlier year than its label; $1.08 billion of that belongs to fiscal 2018 or earlier, before the file starts. Near the turn of a year the number dates a payment to within a few days.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"payee","t":"Payee","type":"org"},{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"type","t":"Kind","type":"text"},{"k":"contract","t":"Contract","type":"text"},{"k":"number","t":"Contract number","type":"text"},{"k":"end","t":"Recorded end of term","type":"date"},{"k":"paidAfter","t":"Paid a year or more after","type":"usd"},{"k":"years","t":"In fiscal years","type":"text"},{"k":"gap","t":"Fiscal years past the end","type":"int"},{"k":"agreed","t":"Agreed amount","type":"usd"},{"k":"signs","t":"Warning signs elsewhere","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"department":"Mayor","contract":"MYR Sunnydale 1A3 Relo & Mega","number":"1000030748","type":"grant","end":"2023-12-31","paidAfter":4867893,"years":"2026","gap":2,"agreed":4888633,"payee":"San Francisco Housing Authority","payeeKey":null,"signs":0},{"department":"City Administrator","contract":"Police Vehicles with Upfitting","number":"1000016950","type":"purchasing","end":"2023-01-15","paidAfter":1581351,"years":"2025, 2026","gap":3,"agreed":9850000,"payee":"Bob Wondries Ford/Wondries Fleet Group","payeeKey":null,"signs":0},{"department":"Mayor","contract":"MYR Treasure Is E1.2Behavioral","number":"1000029993","type":"grant","end":"2024-06-30","paidAfter":1474957,"years":"2026, 2027","gap":3,"agreed":4679657,"payee":"Treasure Island E1.2 Behavioral Health L","payeeKey":null,"signs":0},{"department":"City Administrator","contract":"Tech Mktplc 2.0 Tier 1"